to celebrate the release of Mad Max fury  road let's take a look at 12 things you  probably didn't know about the  post-apocalyptic sequel fury road didn't  start life with a traditional screenplay  instead right and director George Miller  created a visual story with comic book  artist Brenda McCarthy who after seeing  Mad Max 2 in the 80s co-created the  comic freak wave which he's described as  Mad Max go surfing with the help of  storyboard artists Peter pound and Mark  Sexton Miller and McCarthy created the  movies visual first draft a storyboard  with 3,500 panels which covered the  conference room walls of Miller studio  and helped him film and edit the movie  shot by shot before production started  George Miller created an online resource  about the world of fury road for the  movies cast and crew the secure website  included videos of stunts tests costume  fittings and the histories and  hierarchies of each of the movies tribes  in the post-apocalyptic world of fury  road everything has to be reused and  repurposed so to make sure they got the  right look for the movies props the  production design team used real Salvage  materials for example they use soda cans  tires and inner tubes to make weapons  melted down old tank codes and trophies  to make car accessories and 350 salvaged  cars two-part build and customize a host  of vehicles furiosa's mechanical arm was  designed and built by industrial  designer Matt Boog who used salvaged  materials such as spanners parts of car  engines and a motor from a toy airplane  for added mobility a lighter version of  the arm was also produced for Charlize  Theron and her stunt double a core team  of 65 stunt performers brought to life  in wharton joe's army of war boys to  really get into their cultish characters  the stunt performers took part in drama  workshops with actor and fury road  co-writer Nikola Thoris as well as doing  intense fight training  George Miller got the idea for the war  boys aerial attack team the polecats  after seeing street performers balancing  on polls former Cirque de Soleil  performer Stephen bland helped put  together a team of gymnasts to play the  polecats once up in the air  the polecats got their cues via  earpieces and laser pointers on the  vehicles high tensile steel which is  strong but has some flexibility was used  for the polls and each 30-foot pole was  counterweighted with an engine block at  the base which was pushed or pulled by  the vehicle stunt team allowing the  polecats to glide through the air  Australian musician iota who plays the  doof warrior wrote and recorded his own  war tunes which he plays on a double  necked electric guitar as the frontman  of the crazy band whose music rallies  immortan joe's troops to help the bands  taiko drummers keep their tempo amid the  noise of engines and combats the music  was piped to them via earpieces to get a  sense of their personalities George  Miller asked actresses auditioning for  mortem Joe's five wives to read a scene  from any film or TV show  except fury road choices included a  Monty Python scene which was picked by  first-time actress Abby Lee to prepare  to play the five wives Lee and fellow  actors Rosie huntington-whiteley Riley  Keough zoe kravitz and Courtney Eaton  spent three weeks in Sydney rehearsing  and working with fury road co-writer  Nikola Thoris and choreographer Meryl  tankard as background for their  characters they also researched violence  against women in war zones with  playwright Eve Ensler who's worked with  female rape victims in the Congo at the  peak of filming there were over 1700  crew members working on pure erode and  an average of 1000 people on set at any  time the production's base camp was the  size of three football fields and during  120 days moved six times to move gear  from one location to the next they  needed five 8x8 former German military  transport trucks  to create fury roads wasteland the  filmmakers needed a location that was  flat had sand and canyons and little  vegetation originally they wanted to  return to Broken Hill in the Australian  outback where they shot Mad Max 2 but  two years of high rain format the area  was too green however Namibia in  southern Africa had everything they  needed location wise including the  coastal city of swakopmund which  provided the production hub and the  Namib Desert which had the range of  looks required for the road war fury  roads Citadel was actually a series of  sets constructed in the midea Sydney in  Cape Town and its base was built of sand  and fake stone however the Citadel's  towers and surrounding views were  computer-generated the rock walls were  made using high-res photos taken of  Australia's Blue Mountains which were  digitally cursed and bent to form the  shape of the Citadel Australian  motocross champ and freestyle coach  Stephen Gould  helped put together a team of expert  freestylers to play the rock riders who  prowl the canyon a series of ramps and  rollercoasters were built to aid their  movement around the steep canyon walls  filming took place and the midea SWA  Copper River Valley and the size of the  canyon was increased with CGI well there  you have it 12 things you probably  didn't know about Mad Max fury road now  let me know in the comments below what  did you think of the movie and what  would you like to see happen in a sequel  if you enjoyed this video do please  share it hit the thumbs-up button and  subscribe for more things you didn't  know as well as movie reviews and  interviews thanks for watching  yippee-ki-yay movie lovers
 

